  "text": "Here are thirteen datasets. They share their means, their standard deviations and their correlation, to within a hundredth. Now plot them. One is a dinosaur. Others are a star, a circle, and a set of lines. Alberto Cairo drew the dinosaur. In twenty seventeen, Justin Matejka and George Fitzmaurice built the other twelve to match its numbers. A summary is a lossy copy. The plot keeps what it threw away. But a plot makes choices too.\n\nA histogram's bin width is a knob. These are the SAME thousand values, in five bins, then twenty, then eighty. Wide bins blur the shape into a few blocks. Narrow ones turn every wobble into a peak. Nothing in the data says which to believe. So look at more than one.\n\nWhich plot, when? For the shape of one variable, a histogram, at a few bin widths. To read off percentiles with nothing to tune, an ECDF: the share of values at or below each point. To compare groups, box plots. But a box only knows its quartiles, so it can't show a second peak. A violin can. It draws the shape the box leaves out. Whatever you pick, plot FIRST, and summarize second. A summary and a plot describe the data you have. To reason about data you haven't seen, you need probability... starting with what a probability even means.",
